# Routing for the rebuilt lipdverse site.
#
# The rule is one line long and does the whole job: if the request names a file
# or directory that actually exists, serve it; otherwise hand it to the app.
#
# That ordering is deliberate. Every legacy page under data/<datasetId>/<version>
# is a real directory, so it keeps being served exactly as before -- 41,496 of
# them, holding the only record of what past versions contained. Nothing is
# redirected, rewritten or shadowed. The app only ever sees URLs that would
# otherwise have been a 404.
#
# So the transition needs no cutover: a version that exists on disk is history
# and serves itself, a version that does not is rendered live from the export.

RewriteEngine On
RewriteBase /dev/

# Real file or directory wins, always.
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
RewriteRule ^ - [L]

# Never route data out to the app: a missing parquet should 404 as itself
# rather than silently returning an HTML page, which is the failure that makes
# a fetch() error message meaningless.
RewriteRule ^export/ - [L]

# Nor anything that looks like an asset. A missing .js answered with HTML is
# how the app silently failed to start: the browser will not execute text/html
# as a module and reports nothing useful. Let it 404 as itself.
RewriteRule \.(js|mjs|css|map|json|geojson|parquet|duckdb|zip|bib|lpd|csv|png|jpg|svg|ico|woff2?)$ - [L]

# Everything else is the app, which reads the path and decides what to show.
RewriteRule ^ site/index.html [L]

# Parquet served with a sane type so range requests are not second-guessed.
# .lpd is a zip; say so, or a browser may try to render it.
AddType application/zip .lpd
AddType application/x-bibtex .bib
AddType application/geo+json .geojson
AddType application/vnd.apache.parquet .parquet
AddType application/octet-stream .duckdb
